verdicts.reasoning:
I fetched https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing twice on 2026-09-29. The page shows no "last updated" date.

In the Standard text-token table, the fetched rows read "| gpt-4o | $2.50 | $1.25 | - | $10.00 |" and "| gpt-4o-mini | $0.15 | $0.075 | - | $0.60 |". The columns are input, cached input, and output per 1M tokens. The Moderation row reads "| Moderation | omni-moderation-latest | Free |". All three price claims match exactly.

Two things the claim leaves out:
(a) The Batch table halves the gpt-4o price to $1.25 input and $5.00 output. That does not apply here, because every call in the app is synchronous.
(b) Fine-tuned models cost more: gpt-4o-2024-08-06 fine-tuning is $3.75 / $1.875 / $15.00, and gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18 fine-tuning is $0.30 / $0.15 / $1.20. That does not apply either, because model_config.py uses the base model ids.

The B2 part ("Anthropic SDK migration after launch, not before") is a recommendation, not a fact, so a vendor page cannot disprove it. It does agree with the measured facts:
- every LLM call goes through openai.AsyncOpenAI;
- json_object response_format is used at about 4 sites;
- verdict determinism depends on temperature=0;
- content_safety uses client.moderations.create, which is OpenAI-only;
- all quality measurements were taken on gpt-4o and gpt-4o-mini;
- there is no anthropic SDK in requirements.txt;
- there are days until launch, not weeks.

I did not fetch Anthropic's pricing page, so this check relies on no Anthropic prices. The token-count modelling was not re-measured. I called no LLM APIs and no Railway tools.
